I have the transmit-side of a Node.js Kujua Lite "adaptor" module working. There's a browndav/kujua-transport repository (i'll be committing in a second); the actual adaptor implementation is currently in `adaptors/kujua-lite.js`:

``` javascript
/* Test code */
var adaptor = adaptors.kujua_lite.create(null, {
  /* debug: true, password: 'secret', user: 'browndav', ... */
  url: 'http://dev.medicmobile.org:5984/kujua-base_3/_design/kujua-base/_rewrite'
});
adaptor.register_transmit_handler(function (_messages, _callback) {
  console.log('** driver transmit: ', JSON.stringify(_messages));
  return _callback(false, {});
});
```

It's recursively processing SMSsync-style callbacks in both directions.

FWIW, some glue code would ask the Kujua Lite adaptor to tell it when there are pending outbound messages (using `register_transmit_handler`), and would handle the callback by calling the transport API's ->transmit method.

In the other direction, the glue code would call `deliver`, which would push an incoming message in to Kujua Lite, process any necessary HTTP callbacks, and then return control to the glue code so that it can inform the underlying driver that delivery succeeded.

